BOPS dispatches. It is injected verbatim into every phase context by `rbops/dispatch.sh`. It is law, not advice.  You are running inside an automated pipeline. There is no human in your loop. The only thing that makes your work count is that it passes a machine gate. Nothing you *say* about your work is evidence. Only what `rbops/verify.sh` proves is evidence.  ---  ## 0. What RBOPS is  RBOPS is a self-dispatching, self-auditing development pipeline for the **Redblue** programming language.  - **Dispatcher** (`rbops/dispatch.sh`) — picks the next eligible phase from   `rbops/phases.json`, runs it, records state, gates it, triggers the next tick. - **Manifest** (`rbops/phases.json`) — the single source of truth. Machine-readable.   If a phase is not in the manifest, it does not exist. - **Gate** (`rbops/verify.sh`) — the only authority on whether work is real. - **Auditor** (`.opencode/agent/auditor.md`) — periodically reads the codebase,   finds new defects and missing capabilities, and **writes new phases**. - **Bootstrap** — the endgame: Redblue compiles Redblue.  ### The state machine  ```   IDLE ──dispatch──▶ RUNNING ──verify ok──▶ REVIEW ──ok──▶ .done ──▶ next phase                       │                       ├─ verify fail ──▶ RETRY (attempt+1)                       ├─ model error ──▶ .deferred (retry later tick)                       ├─ max attempts ──▶ .blocked (needs human)                       └─ budget spent ──▶ .starved (needs new phases) ```  State is **git**, not a database. Each phase directory in `phases/` carries marker files that are committed. Stateless runners therefore work.  ---  ## 1. HARD RULES — violating any of these fails the phase  1. **Do not touch RBOPS itself.** Never edit `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`,    `rbops/phases.json`, or any `.opencode/agent/*.md`. The bot token has no    `workflows` scope and a push touching `.github/` is refused. Do not waste a    phase discovering this. 2. **Never weaken a gate.** You may not modify `verify.sh`, delete a failing    test, add `#[ignore]`, add `.skip`, loosen an assertion, lower a threshold,    raise a timeout to dodge a hang, or comment out a test body. **Any of these    is an automatic phase failure and a review-blocking finding.** There is no    exception for "it was flaky". Flaky tests get fixed, not muted. 3. **No claiming success you did not achieve.** If `verify.sh` is red, the phase    is not done. Report the failure honestly in `REPORT.md` and exit. 4. **Do not run long builds locally.** CI is the only place `cargo build` runs.    Locally use `cargo check --all-targets` at most, and only if essential. 5. **Never commit secrets, API keys, tokens.** Check `.gitignore` covers    `.env`, `*.key`, `target/`. 6. **No destructive git.** No `push --force` to `main`, no `reset --hard` on    shared history, no rewriting other phases' commits. 7. **One phase, one concern.** If you discover work that does not belong to your    phase, do not do it. Record it in `phases/phase-NNN/FINDINGS.md` so the    auditor can promote it to a real phase. 8. **No unbounded speculation.** Do not rewrite the architecture, rename public    types, change the `.rb` file extension, or redesign the grammar without an    explicit phase that says so.  ---  ## 2. Redblue — what must never silently change  Redblue is a tree-walking interpreter: `Lexer → Parser → Analyzer → VM`. Its contract is *readable English-like source*.  **Language invariants.** A phase may not change these without explicitly re-opening the language design in its own PROMPT:  | Invariant | Why | |---|---| | `.rb` is the source extension | Tooling, `rb run`, LSP | | `to … end`, `if … end`, `for … end` use `end`, never braces | Core philosophy | | `set x to <expr>` is assignment | Grammar | | `say` prints | 100% of examples depend on it | | `Value` variants are `Nothing/Number/Text/YesNo/List/Record/Object/Function/Builtin` | Public API, `redblue::Value` is exported | | Errors are `Error::{Lexer,Parser,Analyzer,Runtime,Io}` | Public API | | Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax | Parser tests | | Existing tests in `tests/` keep passing | — |  **Backwards compatibility.** `examples/*.rb` and `modules/*.rb` are the language's specification-by-example. Every phase must keep them working. The gate runs them.  ---  ## 3. Testing mandate — this is where most phases fail  > Every test must assert something that can fail.  Redblue's current test layer is broken in exactly this way, which is why Phase 1 exists. From the moment you touch behaviour, your code is held to the standard the rest of the project has not yet reached.  ### 3.1 Every test must have  1. **A real assertion.** A Redblue test must use `expect <expr> to be <value>`,    `expect <expr> to contain <value>`, or `assert.equal`. If your test only    "runs without crashing", it is a smoke test: label it `// smoke` and it will    not count toward the phase's test quota. 2. **At least one edge case.** The happy path alone is not a test. 3. **A named failure message.** `expect x to be 5` must be able to fail with a    useful message. 4. **Determinism.** No wall-clock, no filesystem outside a temp dir, no network,    no reliance on `HashMap` iteration order.  ### 3.2 Mandatory edge-case matrix  For **every** behaviour you implement or change, test at minimum:  - **Empty / zero / "nothing"** — empty list, empty text, `nothing`, `0`, `""` - **Singleton and boundary** — exactly one element; index `0` and index `len-1` - **Out of bounds** — index `-1`, index `len`, index `999` (must be a clean   runtime error, never a panic, never UB) - **Type mismatch** — number where text expected, record where list expected - **Type coercion boundaries** — `0/0`, `1/0`, `-0.0`, `NaN`, `±Infinity`,   `2^53±1` (precision), `-2^31`, integer overflow past `i64` - **Unicode and escapes** — empty text, `"`, `\`, newline in string,   emoji/CJK/RTL, combining marks, very long strings - **Nesting and recursion** — nested lists, nested records, 3+ closure/object   scopes, mutual recursion - **Duplicate and missing keys** — record with repeated keys, missing field access - **Malformed input** — unterminated string, unclosed `end`, stray token,   empty file, BOM, CRLF line endings, non-UTF8 bytes - **Resource and state** — file that does not exist, permission denied, path with   spaces, deeply nested call stack, infinite loop guard  Pick the rows that apply. A phase that changes the VM must justify in `REPORT.md` which rows are N/A and why.  ### 3.3 Test quota (hard floor, not a target)  The gate enforces a floor: **≥ 3 new `#[test]` functions or ≥ 2 new Redblue `test` blocks**, plus **≥ 1 test named `edge_*`**, **≥ 1 test that asserts a failure is produced**, and **zero** newly-skipped or newly-ignored tests.  Both mandatory tests must be expressible in whichever language you are working in. Write them like this:  | Requirement | Rust | Redblue | |---|---|---| | edge case test | `fn edge_empty_list() { … }` | `test "edge_empty list is rejected" do` | | asserts a failure | `assert!(r.is_err())` / `#[should_panic]` | `try … catch error … end` |  A Redblue test name is a **string literal**, so it is `test "edge_foo"`, with a quote after `test `. Likewise Redblue has no exceptions: `try … catch error` is its failure-catching construct, and a test that faults without one is not asserting anything. Both forms are recognised by the gate.  **Not every phase has a failure channel.** A linter reports problems as a diagnostics collection; a formatter and an LSP emit documents. There is no `Result::Err` to assert on, so the "asserts a failure is produced" rule cannot apply to them. Phases 015, 016 and 017 declare `failure_assert: "not_applicable"` and the gate **skips that one check and says so**. Every other phase keeps it at full strength — a phase that changes what the interpreter *executes* must prove it produces a clean error when it should.  Deliberately, the count floor is low. It exists to catch "no verification at all", not to judge sufficiency — a hard 6 once failed a genuinely good phase with 5 edge tests, which is the gate being smarter than the reviewer. Judging whether the tests COVER the change is the reviewer's job (AGENTS.md section 5: an untested branch is a MAJOR finding, fixed and re-gated). The gate is the floor — cheap, mechanical, never wrong in the pass direction. The reviewer is the ceiling.  ### 3.5 You must implement, not only test  Most rules in this file measure *verification*. None of them measure *ambition*, so the cheapest way to pass the gate would be to write tests for behaviour that already exists and change no implementation at all. That is not a phase.  Every phase in `rbops/phases.json` from 011 onward declares `must_touch` — the areas where that phase's work must land. The gate fails the phase if **no** changed file falls under any of them:  ```json "must_touch": ["src/"]                 // phases 011-013, 015, 016, 018, 019 "must_touch": ["src/", "modules/"]// phase 014, 024 — either is acceptable "must_touch": ["src/", "bootstrap/"]    // phases 021-023 "must_touch": ["tests/"]                // phase 020 — a harness, test-only by design ```  Read your own phase's entry before you start. If your finding genuinely cannot be fixed in the declared area, that is a manifest bug: say so in `FINDINGS.md` with the file:line, and fix it in the area the finding names rather than bending the phase. Phase-024 is the worked example — its finding cites both `src/lexer.rs` and `modules/MathUtils.rb`, so both are declared and either is a valid answer.  Note what this rule is **not**: it is a floor, satisfied by one changed line under `src/`. It says "you implemented something", not "you implemented enough". Judging that the change is correct, complete and proportionate is the reviewer's job, and an `edge_*` test that does not actually pin the new behaviour is a BLOCKER finding.  ### 3.4 The four gates, in order  ``` cargo fmt --check          # formatting        (fails on any diff) cargo clippy -- -D warnings# lints, zero warnings tolerated cargo test                 # full suite, 0 failures ./rbops/verify.sh          # project-specific gates ```  ---  ## 4. REPORT.md contract — required, machine-checked  `phases/phase-NNN/REPORT.md` must exist. `verify.sh` rejects the phase without it.  ```markdown # Phase NNN — <title>  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/vm.rs | +40 −12 | call depth counter in call_builtin |  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | edge_call_stack_overflow | out of bounds / resource | | test_zero_division | type/numeric boundary |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | cargo fmt --check | pass | | cargo clippy -- -D warnings | pass | | cargo test | 42 passed, 0 failed | | rbops/verify.sh | pass |  ## Invariants touched - None  /  - `expect` now returns a catchable Runtime error (was: process abort)  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - No stack-depth limit for mutually recursive objects yet → FINDINGS.md ```  A REPORT.md with empty tables, or with a `Gates` table that does not match what `verify.sh` printed, is a **fake completion** and will be caught by the auditor.  ---  ## 5. Reviewer contract  The reviewer (`.opencode/agent/reviewer.md`) is **read-only**: no edit, no bash. It reviews the phase diff after the gate is green and emits `FINDINGS:`. Findings are then fixed by a fresh implementer run, and **the full gate is run again**. A phase with open BLOCKER/MAJOR findings is not `.done`.  The reviewer must specifically hunt for:  - **Fake completion** — REPORT.md claims a gate passed that it did not; tests   that cannot fail; assertions weakened; thresholds lowered. - **Panic paths** — any reachable `panic!`/`unwrap`/indexing/`unreachable!`. - **Unbounded resource use** — no recursion limit, no loop guard, no output cap. - **Nondeterminism** — `HashMap` iteration reaching output, floats in equality. - **Error-message regressions** — a previously-rejected program now accepted, or   a crash where an error was expected. - **Spec drift** — behaviour that now contradicts `SPEC.md` or `docs/GRAMMAR.md`.  ---  ## 6. Auditor contract — the phase-generating loop  Every N phases, the **auditor** runs. It must:  1. Measure the codebase: test count, coverage of `src/*.rs`, gate health,    `todo`/`unimplemented`/`FIXME` counts, largest functions, clippy debt. 2. Diff the language's actual behaviour against `SPEC.md` and `ROADMAP.md`    and list every gap as a concrete, file:line-anchored finding. 3. Turn each finding into a **new phase**: id, title, severity, dependencies,    estimated risk, and an acceptance gate — written into `rbops/phases.json`. 4. Refuse to invent work. A phase with no file:line evidence is not created.  The auditor is the only component permitted to append to `phases.json`.  ---  ## 7. Bootstrap contract — the endgame  **Goal:** Redblue compiles Redblue.  This is a ladder, not a leap. Each rung must be green before the next:  | Stage | Artifact | Definition of done | |---|---|---| | **S0** | Rust `rb` (exists today) | `cargo test` green, `rb` runs all `examples/*.rb` | | **S1** | `rb compile` — Redblue **bytecode compiler** written in Rust | emits `.rbc`; `rb vm file.rbc` runs it | | **S2** | The S1 compiler **rewritten in Redblue** | `rb run bootstrap/compiler.rb` produces byte-identical `.rbc` to S1 for a corpus | | **S3** | S2-compiled compiler compiles itself | fixed point: `stage1.rbc == stage2.rbc` byte-for-byte | | **S4** | S3 binary is the shipped `rb` | `rb` built by `rb` passes the full gate |  Non-negotiable rules for any phase in the S2–S4 range:  - **No shimming.** You may not add a Rust-only fast path that the Redblue path   silently falls back to. If it is not implemented in Redblue, it does not exist. - **Fixed-point is the only proof.** "It compiles" is not done. Byte-identical   output over a ≥200-program corpus is done. - **Self-hosting forbids changing the language to make it easier.** Any grammar   change needed for S2+ must be justified by general language merit in its own phase. - **The gate for every bootstrap phase is the S3 fixed-point test**, plus the   normal four gates. No exceptions, no "next phase will finish it".  ---  ## 8. Communication rules  - Report facts, not narrative. "`rbops/verify.sh` reports `edge_foo: FAILED`" beats "there may be an issue with foo". - When blocked, say exactly what you tried and what the exact error was. - Never fabricate a file:line. If you did not read the line, do not cite it. - Prefer the smallest correct change. A 400-line refactor in a bug-fix phase   is a review BLOCKER.  ---  # YOUR PHASE: phase-103  Project root: /home/runner/work/rbops/rbops/redblue  # phase-103 — Add structured logging so programs stop printing to stdout  <!-- GENERATED by rbops/gen-prompts.sh from rbops/phases.json. DO NOT EDIT. -->  | | | |---|---| | Severity | `minor` | | Risk | `low` | | Depends on | phase-090 | | Timeout | 150 min | | Must change | `src/` |  ## You must change `src/`  This phase declares `must_touch: [src/]`. The gate fails the phase unless at least one changed file falls under it. Writing tests, documentation or a report is not enough — if you finish without touching that area, the phase fails on exactly that check.   ## Evidence — verified finding  ``` A program that wants to record what it did has say and console.log and nothing else. There is no level, no destination other than the console, no timestamp attached to a log line, and no way to turn logging off. So a library cannot log without polluting the output of the program using it, and a program that wants diagnostics has to print them where its real output goes - which means a user cannot silence them without editing the source. This is why every Redblue program that has ever needed diagnostics has used a conditional and hoped for the best. ```  **Re-verify this before you change anything.** If the finding no longer reproduces on `main`, stop and write that to `FINDINGS.md` — a stale phase must never be "fixed" by inventing a change.  ## Goal  Add a logging facility with levels, a settable threshold, a settable destination, and a timestamp on each record, so a library can log without speaking to stdout. Make the default level quiet enough that adding a log call cannot change a program's observable output, since a library that changes its caller's output is a defect. Decide whether timestamps make records non-deterministic, and if so keep them out of anything a test would compare - the corpus compares output literally, so a timestamp in the default path would break it.  ## Method  1. Read `AGENTS.md` in full. Obey every hard rule; they are not negotiable. 2. Reproduce the finding. Record the exact command and the exact wrong output. 3. Write the failing test FIRST. Watch it fail. Do not touch production code    until you have a red test that fails for the right reason. 4. Make the smallest change that turns it green. 5. Run the four gates yourself before claiming anything:     ```bash    cargo fmt --all -- --check    cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings    cargo test --all-targets    ./rbops/verify.sh phase-103    ```  6. Write `REPORT.md` in the exact shape required by `AGENTS.md` section 4.  ## Definition of done  - [ ] a record at or above the threshold is emitted and one below it is not, asserted by capturing the destination, for every adjacent pair of levels - [ ] the default level emits nothing, so adding a log call to a library cannot change a program's observable output - asserted by a corpus-style golden comparison - [ ] the destination is settable to a file and a record written there is readable back with the level and message intact - [ ] a timestamp is present on each record and a test proves a deterministic mode exists that omits it, so corpus goldens stay stable - [ ] logging to a closed or unwritable destination raises a catchable error rather than aborting the program - [ ] the tree-walker and the bytecode VM emit equal records, compared as data across both engines - [ ] SPEC.md documents the levels, the default, and the determinism stance - [ ] edge_* tests cover the threshold at its extremes, a record with an empty message, a multi-line message, and concurrent-ish interleaved writes from nested calls  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-103/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 103 — A logging module with levels, destinations and deterministic records  ## What changed | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | `src/logging.rs` | +198 | `Level`, `Destination`, thread-local `Config`, threshold and timestamp setters, record formatting, file writes, and fallible stream writes that report a closed or broken descriptor | | `src/wasm.rs` | +25 −6 | `try_emit`, the fallible form of `emit`, and `is_capturing` so a `stdout` log record stays inside a host capture | | `src/lib.rs` | +1 | Exports `logging` | | `src/stdlib.rs` | +83 | `logging` in `MODULES` and `MODULE_MEMBERS`; nine builtins, names, signatures and effectful entries | | `src/runtime.rs` | +87 | Shared dispatch for `logging.debug/info/warn/error`, `set_level`, `level`, `set_timestamps`, `set_destination` and `reset` | | `tests/stdlib_modules_test.rs` | +512 | Fifteen `logging_module` tests covering thresholds, destinations, timestamps, parity, ordering, closed streams and failures | | `SPEC.md` | +45 | `logging` section: level order, `off` default, destinations, formats, determinism and write failures | | `README.md` | +18 | `logging` quick-reference block and default-behaviour paragraph | | `SURVEY.md` | +47 −47 | Inventory re-anchored to 123 builtins, 15 modules and 101 dotted members; logging gap removed | | `tests/stdlib_module_names_test.rs` | +5 | A documented `logging` call that runs | | `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs` | +57 | Answerable rows for all nine logging builtins | | `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt` | +13 −12 | New SPEC block classified RUNS; shifted SPEC blocks re-anchored | | `tests/doc_blocks_fragments.txt` | +4 −4 | SPEC fragment anchors moved with the new section | | `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt` | +1 −1 | SPEC known-failure anchor moved with the new section | | `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` | +1 | `resume phase-103:` added to the delivery-subject rule |  ## Behaviour `logging.debug`, `logging.info`, `logging.warn` and `logging.error` take one message. A record is written only when its level is at or above the current threshold. The levels are ordered `debug`, `info`, `warn`, `error`; the threshold is one of those four or `off`, and the default is `off`. A library can therefore add logging calls without changing what a program that has not opted in emits.  `logging.set_level` accepts the five threshold names and `logging.set_destination` accepts `stderr`, `stdout`, or any other non-empty text as a file path. `logging.level` reads the threshold back, `logging.set_timestamps` turns timestamps on or off, and `logging.reset` restores `off`, `stderr` and timestamps on. Invalid names, empty destinations, wrong argument types and extra arguments are catchable `Runtime` errors that name the builtin.  The default destination is `stderr`, not the program's normal output stream. A record has the form `[LEVEL] message` in deterministic mode and `[YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.mmmZ] [LEVEL] message` when timestamps are on, always ending with one newline. The timestamp is read from the same clock as `time.now()`, including the playground clock a host supplies through `rb_set_clock`, rather than from a second clock the logging module owns. A host with no clock writes the record without a timestamp rather than failing the run, the same fallback SPEC documents; `time.now()` still answers that there is no clock. An empty or multi-line message is written unchanged. File destinations append one record per write through the same `vfs` path as `files.append`, so a record can be read by either engine. A file that cannot be written raises a catchable `IoError` naming `logging`; it does not abort the process.  The `stdout` and `stderr` destinations are written through a fallible path too, so a descriptor that is closed, read-only, or a pipe with no reader raises the same catchable `IoError` instead of silently losing the record. On Unix the record is written to the descriptor directly because the standard library treats `EBADF` on a standard stream as success; a `stdout` record stays inside a host capture only when the calling thread belongs to the run that opened it, so one run's logging cannot be collected by another run's capture.  The configuration is thread-local and both engines dispatch through the same `runtime::builtin` arms. There is no second formatter or threshold check in the bytecode VM, so the same program produces the same records under both engines.  ## Tests added | Test | Edge class covered | |---|---| | `records_at_or_above_the_threshold_reach_a_file_destination` | happy path; below, at and above the threshold | | `edge_every_adjacent_threshold_pair_filters_exactly` | every adjacent pair plus `off` | | `default_logging_emits_nothing_into_a_programs_output` | default `off`; no file, no stdout, no stderr record | | `edge_the_stdout_destination_joins_the_programs_captured_output` | explicit stdout destination and captured output | | `edge_the_default_destination_is_stderr_not_stdout` | native CLI split between stdout and stderr | | `a_record_carries_a_timestamp_unless_deterministic_mode_omits_it` | timestamp shape and deterministic mode | | `the_tree_walker_and_the_bytecode_vm_emit_equal_records` | cross-engine output and cross-engine files | | `edge_empty_and_multiline_messages_are_written_unchanged` | empty message, embedded newline and trailing newline | | `edge_nested_calls_interleave_records_in_call_order` | nested calls and stable ordering | | `edge_an_unwritable_destination_is_a_catchable_error` | missing directory, `IoError`, both engines | | `edge_invalid_levels_and_destinations_are_refused` | bad level, empty destination, wrong types and arity | | `edge_a_closed_stderr_is_a_catchable_error` | a read-only standard stream, `IoError`, not a silent drop | | `edge_a_closed_stdout_is_a_catchable_error` | a read-only output stream, `IoError`, caught and recorded | | `edge_a_readerless_pipe_is_a_catchable_error` | a pipe with no reader, `EPIPE`, caught and recorded | | `edge_a_directory_destination_is_a_catchable_error` | a directory as a file destination, both engines | | `edge_an_unrelated_thread_is_not_part_of_an_open_capture` | capture membership is scoped to the run that opened it, not the process |  ## Mandatory edge-case matrix | Row | Status | |---|---| | empty | covered — an empty message writes an empty body; an empty destination is refused | | singleton | covered — one record at the threshold is written once, and one below it writes nothing | | boundary | covered — every adjacent threshold pair, the highest level and `off` | | out_of_bounds | covered — an unknown level and a destination whose directory does not exist, or is a directory, are refused | | type_mismatch | covered — a number where a level or destination is wanted, a non-boolean timestamp switch, and extra arguments to `level`/`reset` | | numeric_boundary | covered — a number is not accepted as a level or destination; logging performs no numeric formatting | | unicode | N/A — messages are opaque text passed through unchanged; this facility adds no Unicode-specific boundary | | nesting_recursion | covered — a function called inside another writes its records in call order | | duplicate_missing_keys | N/A — logging has no user-supplied keys or collections | | malformed_input | covered — unknown level, empty destination, missing message and wrong argument types | | resource_limit | covered — a closed, read-only or readerless stream and an unwritable file are catchable `IoError`s, not aborts or silent drops |  ## Gates | Gate | Result | |---|---| | `cargo fmt --all -- --check` | pass | | `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` | pass | | `cargo test --all-targets` | pass — 79 test binaries, 2502 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored | | `../rbops/verify.sh "phase-103"` | pass |  ## Invariants touched - `stdlib::MODULES` gains `logging`; `MODULE_MEMBERS` gains its nine documented   members. The analyzer's existing dotted-call path needs no grammar change. - `BUILTIN_NAMES`, `BUILTIN_SIGNATURES` and `EFFECTFUL_BUILTINS` gain the nine   `logging_*` entries, so documentation, reachability and effect auditing agree   with dispatch. - No lexer, parser, analyzer, `Value` or bytecode instruction changed. Both   engines already call the shared `runtime::builtin` function. - The default is deliberately inert: threshold `off`, destination `stderr`, and   timestamps on. A program must enable a level before any record exists. - No new dependency: timestamps use `chrono`, file writes use the existing `vfs`   abstraction, and the Unix descriptor write uses the existing `libc`   target-dependency. `say`/`print` still go through `emit` unchanged; only   logging uses the fallible `try_emit`. - No test was skipped, ignored or weakened, and no lint suppression was added.  ## Existing test reconciliation - `tests/stdlib_module_names_test.rs` gains the `logging` module and a call that   answers `off` before any configuration. The existing bidirectional module and   member checks then cover the new surface. - `tests/stdlib_module_docs_test.rs` gains an answerable program row for every   `logging_*` builtin. - `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt` classifies the new `SPEC.md:1231` block as RUNS and   re-anchors later SPEC blocks whose line numbers moved. The untouched README   example uses the document's existing plain fence, so it is not a   `redblue`-tagged block for the executor. - `tests/doc_blocks_fragments.txt` and `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt` were   re-anchored, not reclassified. The phase-088 scanner exposed five logging   tests whose only success check was `is_ok()`; each now also compares the   program's captured output, so the scanner remains at zero vacuous tests. - `SURVEY.md` now states 123 builtins, 15 modules and 101 dotted members, lists   the logging row, removes the old "no logging" gap, and re-anchors citations   displaced by the new registry entries. - `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` gains the `resume phase-103:` delivery   subject. The rule already covers every phase in flight; this phase's delivery   commit was the one subject it had not been told about yet. No entry was   removed and no assertion was loosened.  ## Known gaps / follow-ups - Configuration is thread-local. That isolates tests and matches the   single-threaded CLI, but two host threads do not share a threshold or   destination. - There is no rotation, size limit, retention policy, file locking or structured   field support. Each enabled call appends one record. - The timestamp format is fixed: UTC with millisecond precision. There is no   custom format, local timezone, or caller-supplied clock. - `stdout` and `stderr` are reserved destination names, so a file literally   named `stdout` or `stderr` cannot be selected without a path prefix. - There is no per-module logger or level filter; one threshold and one   destination apply to every call in the thread.  ```
